  <!-- 2. What is PerfX Studio? -->
  <div id="hacc-overview" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-overview')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M12 16v-5M12 8h.01"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">What is PerfX Studio?</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Four script tools + the Results Analyser · any input → VuGen script</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <p class="help-p">PerfX Studio is a four-tool platform that converts any performance testing input — Postman/Bruno collections, JMeter test plans, or browser recordings — into production-ready VuGen scripts in minutes. It handles the work that normally takes days: correlation, parameterisation, authentication, and transaction boundaries. The fifth tool, the Results Analyser, turns the raw results of a LoadRunner Enterprise run into a clear report.</p>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Tool</th>
              <th>What you give it</th>
              <th>What you get</th>
              <th>Best for</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-convert)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2 2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h1M16 3h1a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2 2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2h-1"/></svg>Postman / Bruno Converter</td><td>.json / .yml / .bru collection</td><td>VuGen script ZIP</td><td>Teams already using Postman or Bruno</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-jmeter)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M17 3l4 4-4 4"/><path d="M3 7h18"/><path d="M7 21l-4-4 4-4"/><path d="M21 17H3"/></svg>JMeter Converter</td><td>.jmx file + optional CSVs</td><td>VuGen script ZIP + Workload Excel</td><td>Migrating from JMeter to LoadRunner</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-record)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3.5" fill="currentColor"/></svg>Recorder</td><td>Browser HAR file (F12 export)</td><td>VuGen script ZIP</td><td>Virtual desktops and Azure machines where VuGen can't record</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-studio)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg>Script Studio</td><td>1 or 2 HAR files</td><td>Fully correlated VuGen script ZIP</td><td>Any app with tokens, session IDs, or CSRF values</td></tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-results)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 3v18h18"/><path d="M7 15v-4M11 15V8M15 15v-6M19 15V5"/></svg>Results Analyser</td>
              <td>LRE raw results folder or ZIP</td>
              <td>Excel, Word, PDF and HTML report</td>
              <td>Understanding a test run and sharing the results</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-blue">
          <strong><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg>Privacy first.</strong> Everything you upload is processed in-memory and never written to disk. Nothing is stored between requests. Download links expire after 5 minutes and are single-use.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="help-group-h">Tools</h2>

  <!-- 4. Postman / Bruno Converter -->
  <div id="hacc-converter" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-convert)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-converter')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-convert)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2 2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h1M16 3h1a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2 2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2h-1"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Postman / Bruno Converter</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Upload a collection → get a VuGen script ZIP</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-blue"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2 2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h1M16 3h1a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2 2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2h-1"/></svg>Postman / Bruno Converter</div>
        <p class="help-p">Convert your API collection directly into a VuGen script — no scripting knowledge required. The converter reads all requests, headers, bodies, auth, and pre/post scripts, and generates a complete VuGen project.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Supported input formats</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>Postman v2.1</strong> — export from Postman: <em>Collection → ⋯ → Export → Collection v2.1</em></li>
          <li><strong>Bruno JSON</strong> — the <code>.json</code> export from Bruno desktop</li>
          <li><strong>Bruno YAML</strong> — <code>.yml</code> or <code>.yaml</code> collection files</li>
          <li><strong>Bruno folder</strong> — a zipped directory of <code>.bru</code> files</li>
          <li><strong>Single .bru file</strong> — a single Bruno request</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="help-p">Optionally upload an <strong>Environment file</strong> alongside your collection. It fills in variable values — base URLs, API keys, usernames — directly in the generated script so you do not need to edit them by hand.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Step-by-step</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>Click <strong>Postman / Bruno</strong> in the sidebar.</li>
          <li>Choose your output format: <strong>DevWeb</strong> (JavaScript) or <strong>Web HTTP/HTML</strong> (C).</li>
          <li>Drag and drop your collection file into the upload area, or click to browse.</li>
          <li>Optionally upload an environment file in the second upload area.</li>
          <li>Adjust options as needed: think time, transactions, correlation toggles.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Convert</strong> and wait a few seconds.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Download ZIP</strong> when it appears.</li>
          <li>Extract the ZIP and open the <code>.usr</code> file in VuGen, or upload it to LoadRunner Enterprise.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-blue">
          <strong>Multi-script mode:</strong> Enable this when your collection has multiple folders. Instead of one large script, you get one separate script per folder — ideal for modular test design.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. JMeter Converter -->
  <div id="hacc-jmeter" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-jmeter)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-jmeter')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-jmeter)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M17 3l4 4-4 4"/><path d="M3 7h18"/><path d="M7 21l-4-4 4-4"/><path d="M21 17H3"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">JMeter Converter</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Migrate .jmx test plans to VuGen + Workload Excel</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-orange"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M17 3l4 4-4 4"/><path d="M3 7h18"/><path d="M7 21l-4-4 4-4"/><path d="M21 17H3"/></svg>JMeter Converter</div>
        <p class="help-p">Migrating from Apache JMeter to LoadRunner? Upload your <code>.jmx</code> test plan and receive an equivalent VuGen script, plus a <strong>Workload Model Excel</strong> file that translates your JMeter thread groups into a LoadRunner workload definition.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">What to upload</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>.jmx file</strong> — your JMeter test plan (exported from JMeter: <em>File → Save Test Plan As</em>)</li>
          <li><strong>CSV files</strong> — any data files referenced by CSVDataSet elements in the plan (optional but recommended)</li>
          <li><strong>Certificate files</strong> — client certificates (.pem / .p12) used for mTLS authentication (optional)</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Step-by-step</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>Click <strong>JMeter</strong> in the sidebar.</li>
          <li>Choose your output format: <strong>DevWeb</strong> or <strong>Web HTTP/HTML</strong>.</li>
          <li>Upload your <code>.jmx</code> file. Optionally add CSV and certificate files.</li>
          <li>Select script mode: <strong>Single script</strong> or <strong>Per thread group</strong>.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Convert</strong>.</li>
          <li>Download the ZIP — it includes VuGen scripts and a Workload Model Excel file.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-amber">
          <strong>Per-thread-group mode:</strong> Each JMeter Thread Group becomes its own VuGen script. Recommended when thread groups represent different user roles or business flows.
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">What gets converted</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li>HTTP Sampler requests → VuGen web requests with headers and bodies</li>
          <li>CSVDataSet → VuGen parameterisation with matching data files</li>
          <li>Extractors (JSON, RegEx, XPath, Boundary) → equivalent VuGen correlation code</li>
          <li>setUp / tearDown Thread Groups → <code>vuser_init.c</code> / <code>vuser_end.c</code></li>
          <li>JSR223 Groovy scripts → converted where possible, flagged where manual review needed</li>
          <li>Thread Group settings → Workload Model Excel (ramp, users, duration)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 6. Recorder -->
  <div id="hacc-recorder" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-record)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-recorder')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-record)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3.5" fill="currentColor"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Recorder</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Browser HAR → VuGen script · works when VuGen recording is blocked</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-green"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3.5" fill="currentColor"/></svg>Recorder</div>
        <p class="help-p">In a standard workflow, VuGen records user journeys directly via its built-in proxy. However, on some corporate virtual desktops and Azure virtual machines, VuGen recording does not function due to security policies and incomplete installations. The Recorder addresses this by capturing network traffic through your browser's Developer Tools (F12) as a HAR file, then converting it into a structured VuGen script.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">What is a HAR file?</h3>
        <p class="help-p">A HAR (HTTP Archive) file is a recording of all the network requests your browser made during a session. Every modern browser's Developer Tools (F12) can export one. It contains all the URLs, request headers, request bodies, and responses for everything that happened during your recording.</p>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-green">
          <strong>Two ways to record — choose what works for you:</strong><br><br>
          <strong>Option A — CDP Recorder (recommended if extensions are blocked):</strong> A floating toolbar, no browser extension install at all — works on locked-down corporate machines where "Load unpacked" is disabled by Group Policy. One-time setup: download the ZIP, extract it, then run cdp-recorder.exe. Click Start, record your journey (including new tabs and popups), click Stop — the HAR downloads straight from the toolbar.
          <a href="<%= basePath %>/downloads/cdp-recorder">Get CDP Recorder →</a><br><br>
          <strong>Option B — Classic Bookmarklet:</strong> No install at all. Drag two buttons to your bookmark bar, open DevTools (F12), record your journey, export the HAR manually, then upload it here.
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Step-by-step (Classic — bookmarklet method)</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>Open <strong>Recorder</strong> in the sidebar and follow the <em>Bookmarks + DevTools</em> instructions to install the recording helper in your browser bookmarks bar. You only need to do this once.</li>
          <li>Open your browser and navigate to the application you want to test.</li>
          <li>Open <strong>DevTools</strong> (press F12) and go to the <strong>Network</strong> tab. Make sure recording is on (the red circle should be active).</li>
          <li>Perform your complete user journey — log in, navigate pages, submit forms — exactly as a real user would.</li>
          <li>When done, in DevTools Network tab click the <strong>Export HAR</strong> button (the download icon) and save the <code>.har</code> file.</li>
          <li>Come back to the Recorder tool and drag the <code>.har</code> file into the drop zone, or click to browse.</li>
          <li>Use the <strong>Domain Filter</strong> to uncheck domains you do not want in your script (e.g. analytics, CDNs, fonts).</li>
          <li>Drag request rows to group them into named <strong>transactions</strong> if needed.</li>
          <li>Choose your output format (<strong>DevWeb</strong> or <strong>Web HTTP/HTML</strong>) and click <strong>Generate Script</strong>.</li>
          <li>Download the ZIP and open it in VuGen.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-blue">
          <strong>Tip:</strong> For deeper correlation (auto-detection of dynamic values like tokens and session IDs), take your HAR file into <strong>Script Studio</strong>. Record the same journey twice and upload both HAR files to Script Studio for the most complete result.
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Static assets — should I include them?</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>You want</th>
              <th>Do this</th>
              <th>On replay</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Server / API load</td><td>Leave <strong>Static Assets</strong> ticked (default)</td><td>Only images and stylesheets named in the page HTML are downloaded. Script files, and files that JavaScript or CSS requests (fonts, lazy-loaded code, images on later screens, analytics), are not.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Realistic page load, or a <strong>JavaScript-based app</strong> (React, Angular, Vue&hellip;)</td><td>Untick <strong>Static Assets</strong> (and <strong>Analytics/Ads</strong> if wanted)</td><td>Images and stylesheets in the HTML still download automatically; script files and files loaded by JavaScript/CSS are listed on their page step. Nothing is downloaded twice.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="help-p">Why: <code>downloadHtmlStaticResources</code> (DevWeb) and <code>Mode=HTML</code> (VuGen) read the page&#x2019;s HTML text and download what it names &#x2014; they do not run the page&#x2019;s JavaScript. Keep both on in every script. Included files are never separate requests: they are added to the page step that loaded them (VuGen <code>EXTRARES</code>, DevWeb <code>resources: [...]</code>).</p>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-amber">
          <strong>Not sure which kind your app is?</strong> Generate once with static assets included and read the comment above each page step, e.g. <code>// Page resources: 15 images/stylesheets in the page HTML download automatically; 2 other (scripts, CSS/JS-loaded files) are listed</code>. If many are listed, keep them included. VuGen also needs the run-time setting <strong>Download non-HTML resources</strong> switched on (it is on by default).<br><br>
          <strong>Never scripted:</strong> failed or cancelled loads, repeats of the same file on a page (counted in the comment as &#x201C;failed or repeated&#x201D;) and <code>data:</code> / <code>blob:</code> URLs. Files the browser reused from its cache on a later page are still listed on that page, as VuGen records them. Analytics beacons sent with POST stay as their own request. Iframes named in the page HTML download automatically with the page. In a transaction with no page load (single-page-app screens that only call APIs), resources are listed on the API request in progress.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 7. Script Studio -->
  <div id="hacc-studio" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-studio)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-studio')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-studio)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Script Studio</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">HAR correlation engine · tokens, sessions, CSRF auto-detected</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-purple"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg>Script Studio</div>
        <p class="help-p">Script Studio takes HAR files and generates VuGen scripts with <strong>correlation</strong> already built in. Correlation means the script automatically captures dynamic values (like login tokens, session IDs, CSRF tokens, and order IDs) from server responses and reuses them in later requests — just like a real user's browser does.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">What is correlation and why does it matter?</h3>
        <p class="help-p">When you log in to a web application, the server sends back a token or session ID. Every request after that must include this token. If your script has the token hard-coded from your recording session, it will fail when run again because the server issues a new token each time. Correlation solves this by extracting the token dynamically at runtime and passing it forward automatically.</p>
        <p class="help-p">Without correlation, your script will fail on replay. Script Studio generates the extraction and substitution code for you automatically.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">1 HAR vs 2 HARs</h3>
        <div class="help-formats">
          <div class="help-format-card help-format-purple1">
            <div class="help-format-title">1 HAR file</div>
            <p class="help-format-desc">Pattern-based analysis to find values that look dynamic (UUIDs, tokens, long random strings, timestamps). Fast and works well for simple applications.</p>
            <p class="help-format-rec"><strong>Use when:</strong> you have one recording and want a quick result. Some correlations may need manual adjustment.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="help-format-card help-format-purple2">
            <div class="help-format-title">2 HAR files (recommended)</div>
            <p class="help-format-desc">Record the exact same user journey twice with different data (e.g. two different logins). Script Studio compares both and finds every value that changed — guaranteed to be dynamic. Far more complete and accurate correlation.</p>
            <p class="help-format-rec"><strong>Use when:</strong> you want the most reliable script. Recommended for any app with login, tokens, or session management.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Step-by-step</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>Use the <strong>Recorder</strong> tool (or browser DevTools directly) to create your HAR file(s).</li>
          <li>For 2-HAR mode: record the same journey twice using different test credentials or data.</li>
          <li>Open <strong>Script Studio</strong> from the sidebar.</li>
          <li>Drag your first HAR file into the <strong>Recording 1</strong> drop zone. For 2-HAR mode, drag the second into <strong>Recording 2</strong>.</li>
          <li>Choose your output format: <strong>DevWeb</strong>, <strong>Web HTTP/HTML</strong>, or <strong>Both</strong>.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Analyze</strong>.</li>
          <li>Review the correlation and parameterization results shown.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Download ZIP</strong> and open the script in VuGen.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Correlation Advisor</h3>
        <p class="help-p">After analysis, the <strong>Correlation Advisor</strong> panel appears at the top of the results. It lists every dynamic value it found — each as a card showing confidence level, where the value came from, and where it is used. Review each card and click <strong>Accept</strong> to add it as a correlation, then click <strong>▶ Apply &amp; Regenerate</strong> to rebuild the script.</p>
        <p class="help-p">You can add your own correlations manually (click <strong>+ Add Manual</strong>) or use <strong>Paste &amp; Detect</strong> when you know the value but it is not visible in the HAR.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Request Inspector — Traffic Light Highlighting</h3>
        <p class="help-p">Expand the <strong>Request Inspector</strong> inside the Advisor panel and select any request from the dropdown. Every field in that request is colour-coded:</p>
        <ul class="help-steps-list">
          <li><span class="help-light help-light-ok">● Green</span> — value traced to a prior server response. Click <strong>Trace</strong> to see which request produced it, or <strong>+ Correlate</strong> to add it in one click.</li>
          <li><span class="help-light help-light-warn">● Yellow</span> — looks dynamic (JWT, UUID, or token pattern) but its source was not found in the HAR. Use <strong>Paste &amp; Detect</strong> to create the extractor manually.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="help-p">The inspector scans request headers, JSON body, form parameters, query string, <strong>and URL path segments</strong> — so a session ID embedded in a REST URL like <code>/api/users/286522</code> is detected just like any other field.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Paste &amp; Detect</h3>
        <p class="help-p">Click <strong>Paste &amp; Detect</strong> in the Advisor header. Paste the server response body and the exact value you want to extract, then click <strong>Detect &amp; Generate</strong>. The tool automatically picks the best extractor (JSONPath for JSON, Boundary for HTML/XML, RegExp for JWT / UUID / hex tokens) and shows radio buttons or occurrence dropdowns when values appear multiple times.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Extractor types — how they are chosen</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Extractor</th>
              <th>When used</th>
              <th>DevWeb function</th>
              <th>VuGen C function</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">JSONPath</td><td>JSON response bodies</td><td><code>JsonPathExtractor</code></td><td><code>web_reg_save_param_json</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Boundary</td><td>HTML, XML, plain text</td><td><code>BoundaryExtractor</code></td><td><code>web_reg_save_param</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">RegExp</td><td>JWT, UUID, hex, token shapes</td><td><code>RegExpExtractor</code></td><td><code>web_reg_save_param_regexp</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Header</td><td>Response header value</td><td><code>BoundaryExtractor</code> (Headers scope)</td><td><code>web_reg_save_param</code> (Search=Headers)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Request Filters</h3>
        <p class="help-p">Filters run <em>before</em> analysis. By default only <strong>Fetch/XHR</strong> and <strong>Document</strong> requests are included — CSS, JS, fonts, images and media are left out, which suits server / API load. Select their types (or <strong>All</strong>) for realistic page load, and always for <strong>JavaScript-based apps</strong>: files that JavaScript or CSS requests are never in the page HTML, so <code>downloadHtmlStaticResources</code> / <code>Mode=HTML</code> cannot download them on their own. Included files are added to the page step that loaded them (<code>EXTRARES</code> / <code>resources: [...]</code>), never as separate requests. Known analytics/ads domains are always left out in Script Studio. See <strong>Recorder → Static assets — should I include them?</strong> for the full guidance.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 7b. Results Analyser -->
  <div id="hacc-analyser" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-results)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-analyser')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-results)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 3v18h18"/><path d="M7 15v-4M11 15V8M15 15v-6M19 15V5"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Results Analyser</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">LRE raw results → transaction summary, steady state, insights · Excel / Word / PDF</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-purple"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 3v18h18"/><path d="M7 15v-4M11 15V8M15 15v-6M19 15V5"/></svg>Results Analyser</div>
        <p class="help-p">Analyses a LoadRunner Enterprise run from its <strong>raw results</strong>, independently of LRE Analysis. The files are read by your browser on your PC — <strong>nothing is uploaded or stored on the server</strong>, whatever the size of the run.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">How to use it</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>In LRE, download the run's <strong>raw results</strong> (the ZIP that contains <code>RawData.eve</code>, <code>_t_rep.eve</code> and <code>res*.lrr</code>).</li>
          <li>Open <strong>Results</strong> in the menu and choose the ZIP — or, faster for very large runs, unzip it and choose the folder.</li>
          <li>Review the tabs: <strong>Overview</strong>, <strong>Transactions</strong> (full run and steady state), <strong>Charts</strong>, <strong>Errors</strong>, <strong>Workload</strong>, <strong>Insights</strong> and <strong>Validation</strong>.</li>
          <li>Optional: change the <strong>analysis window</strong> (default = detected steady state) and enter <strong>SLA</strong> thresholds — all figures and insights update.</li>
          <li>Export <strong>Excel</strong> (9 sheets), <strong>Word</strong> (editable report) or <strong>PDF</strong> (print dialog → Save as PDF).</li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="help-h3">How the numbers are computed</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>Response times</strong> come from every transaction sample in <code>RawData.eve</code>. Percentiles are exact (nearest-rank) for runs up to 20 million samples, and within 0.5 % above that.</li>
          <li><strong>Steady state</strong> = from the moment the last VUser starts running to the moment the first VUser exits (from <code>_t_rep.eve</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Pass / fail</strong> counts come from LRE's own sum_data graphs, used only when they reproduce the RawData.eve counts.</li>
          <li><strong>Insights</strong> are offline statistical checks: pacing and Little's Law, trend (Mann-Kendall), change points, spikes (median/MAD), load-generator comparison, LG CPU and memory, errors, SLA and Apdex.</li>
          <li>The <strong>Validation</strong> tab lists every self-check. Transaction counts should match LRE Analysis exactly; percentiles may differ slightly because LRE interpolates.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Very large runs</h3>
        <p class="help-p">Any size works in the browser because files are streamed. For runs of hundreds of GB, the same engine runs from the command line on a machine that has the files: <code>node lre-results-analyser/cli/analyse.js "D:ResultsRun_59" --xlsx run59.xlsx --html run59.html</code>. Add <code>--redact</code> to remove host names and IP addresses before sharing the output.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="help-group-h">Capabilities</h2>

  <!-- 3. Output Formats -->
  <div id="hacc-formats" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-formats')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h10a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><path d="M14 3v5h5"/><path d="m10 13-2 2 2 2M14 13l2 2-2 2"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Output Formats</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">DevWeb JavaScript vs Web HTTP/HTML C — which to choose?</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <p class="help-p">All four tools can generate scripts in either of two formats:</p>
        <div class="help-formats">
          <div class="help-format-card help-format-blue">
            <div class="help-format-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h10a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><path d="M14 3v5h5"/><path d="m10 13-2 2 2 2M14 13l2 2-2 2"/></svg>DevWeb · JavaScript</div>
            <p class="help-format-desc">The modern LoadRunner protocol. Scripts are written in JavaScript and run in a Node.js-like environment. Generates a <code>main.js</code> file.</p>
            <p class="help-format-rec"><strong>Choose this if:</strong> your team uses LRE 2021 or later, or you are comfortable with JavaScript.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="help-format-card help-format-orange">
            <div class="help-format-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h10a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><path d="M14 3v5h5"/><path d="m10 13-2 2 2 2M14 13l2 2-2 2"/></svg>Web HTTP/HTML · C</div>
            <p class="help-format-desc">The classic VuGen protocol. Scripts are written in C and use familiar VuGen functions like <code>web_url()</code> and <code>web_custom_request()</code>. Generates an <code>Action.c</code> file.</p>
            <p class="help-format-rec"><strong>Choose this if:</strong> your team uses older LRE versions or prefers the traditional VuGen scripting style.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="help-p">Not sure? Ask your LRE administrator which protocol your project uses. When in doubt, choose <strong>DevWeb</strong> — it is the current default for new projects.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 8. Capabilities -->
  <div id="hacc-capabilities" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-capabilities')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 21v-7M4 10V3M12 21v-9M12 8V3M20 21v-5M20 12V3M1 14h6M9 8h6M17 16h6"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Capabilities</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Auth methods, correlation, parameterisation — all handled automatically</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <p class="help-p">The toolkit detects and generates code for the following — no manual configuration needed.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Authentication</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Method</th>
              <th>What the toolkit does</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">OAuth2</td><td>Client Credentials, Password Grant, Authorization Code flows</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">JWT</td><td>Detects signing libraries (jsrsasign, jose, JJWT, nimbus), extracts claim mappings, generates token creation code</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">DPoP</td><td>RFC 9449 – generates unique cryptographic proofs per request using EC P-256 keys. Detected from <code>dpop</code> / <code>dpop-pf</code> headers.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">PKCE <span class="help-new">NEW</span></td><td>RFC 7636 – generates <code>code_verifier</code> + <code>code_challenge</code> per iteration. Detected from <code>code_challenge</code> in auth URLs and <code>code_verifier</code> in token exchange bodies.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Kerberos / NTLM</td><td>Windows SSO – generates <code>web_set_user()</code> in VuGen, <code>setUserCredentials()</code> in DevWeb, configures runtime settings</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Basic / Bearer / API Key</td><td>Username/password, static or correlated tokens, header-based or query parameter keys</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">AWS Signature v4</td><td>AWS API request signing</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">mTLS</td><td>Client certificate authentication – upload <code>.pem</code> or <code>.p12</code> files alongside your collection</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Analysis &amp; optimisation</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>Correlation</strong> – finds dynamic values (tokens, session IDs, CSRF tokens) in responses and extracts them for reuse in later requests</li>
          <li><strong>3-tier parameterisation</strong> – classifies variables as Dynamic (runtime), Config (environment), or Test Data (per-user)</li>
          <li><strong>Server host detection</strong> – parameterises hostnames so you can switch environments (dev/test/prod) by changing one value</li>
          <li><strong>Redirect detection</strong> – identifies HTTP 301/302/307 redirects that VuGen follows automatically and omits them from the script</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Accepted input formats</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Format</th>
              <th>Source</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Postman Collection v2.1</td><td><code>.json</code> – Postman → Export → Collection v2.1</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Bruno Collection</td><td><code>.json</code> / <code>.yml</code> / <code>.bru</code> / folder (zipped)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">JMeter Test Plan</td><td><code>.jmx</code> – with optional CSV data files and certificates</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">HAR file</td><td><code>.har</code> – any browser → F12 → Network → Export HAR</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Chrome NetLog</td><td><code>.json</code> – chrome://net-export/ (no POST bodies — for pop-ups and new tabs, prefer the CDP Recorder)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="help-group-h">Reference</h2>

  <!-- 9. Privacy & Security -->
  <div id="hacc-privacy" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-privacy')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Privacy &amp; Security</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">In-memory processing · no disk writes · no data retained</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-green">
          <strong><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg>Designed for regulated environments.</strong> PerfX Studio processes all uploaded files in-memory and never writes data to disk. No data persists between requests. This design was chosen specifically to suit enterprise and banking environments where data handling policies are strict.
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Data handling — what happens to your files</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Question</th>
              <th>Answer</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Is my file saved to disk?</td><td><strong class="help-yes">No.</strong> Files are received directly into memory (RAM). No temporary files are created on disk at any point.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Is my data stored between requests?</td><td><strong class="help-yes">No.</strong> Each conversion runs in its own isolated in-memory context. When your request ends, all data is released.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Can anyone else see my upload?</td><td><strong class="help-yes">No.</strong> Data lives only in your request's memory scope. Other users cannot access it, and server logs do not contain file contents.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Are external services called?</td><td><strong class="help-yes">No.</strong> All processing is local. No data leaves the server other than the ZIP file returned to you.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">How long does the download link last?</td><td>Download tokens expire after <strong>5 minutes</strong> and are single-use. Once clicked, the token is invalidated. Run the conversion again if it expires — it takes only a few seconds.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Do HAR files contain sensitive data?</td><td>Yes — HAR files contain all request headers, cookies, and response bodies from your recorded session. This may include session tokens, API keys, or personal data. Treat your HAR files as sensitive, use them only on trusted machines, and delete them after conversion.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Technical stack</h3>
        <div class="help-cards">
          <div class="help-card" style="--c-tool:var(--c-convert)">
            <div class="help-card-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="7" rx="2"/><rect x="3" y="13" width="18" height="7" rx="2"/><path d="M7 7.5h.01M7 16.5h.01"/></svg>Server</div>
            <p class="help-card-p"><strong>Node.js</strong> + <strong>Express</strong>. File uploads handled by <strong>Multer</strong> with in-memory storage — no disk writes. File size limits enforced at the upload boundary. Served on your internal network only.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="help-card" style="--c-tool:var(--c-studio)">
            <div class="help-card-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg>Crypto library</div>
            <p class="help-card-p"><code>lre-utils.dat</code> — zero-dependency crypto runtime for VuGen scripts. Handles EC P-256 key gen, ECDSA signing, RSA-PSS (PS256), SHA-256, PKCE pair generation. DevWeb uses Node.js built-in <code>crypto</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 10. Glossary -->
  <div id="hacc-glossary" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-glossary')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20V3H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 0 4 5.5z"/><path d="M4 19.5V21h16"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Glossary</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">VuGen, LRE, HAR, correlation, JWT, DPoP, PKCE and more</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <dl class="help-glossary">
          <dt>VuGen</dt>
          <dd>Virtual User Generator — the tool in LoadRunner Enterprise used to create and edit performance test scripts that simulate real users.</dd>
          <dt>LRE / LoadRunner Enterprise</dt>
          <dd>The performance testing platform. Scripts created by this toolkit are designed to run on LRE.</dd>
          <dt>DevWeb</dt>
          <dd>A modern LoadRunner protocol where scripts are written in JavaScript. Scripts run as <code>main.js</code>.</dd>
          <dt>Web HTTP/HTML (C)</dt>
          <dd>The classic LoadRunner protocol where scripts are written in C. Scripts run as <code>Action.c</code>.</dd>
          <dt>HAR file</dt>
          <dd>HTTP Archive — a JSON file exported from your browser's Developer Tools that contains a complete recording of all network requests made during a session.</dd>
          <dt>Correlation</dt>
          <dd>The process of capturing a dynamic value (like a token or session ID) from a server response and automatically inserting it into later requests, so the script works correctly on every run.</dd>
          <dt>Parameterization</dt>
          <dd>Replacing a hard-coded value (like a username or password) in a script with a variable that reads from a data file, so different virtual users use different data.</dd>
          <dt>Transaction</dt>
          <dd>A named measurement boundary in a VuGen script. LoadRunner records response time for each transaction separately, so you can see performance for specific user actions (e.g. "Login", "Search", "Checkout").</dd>
          <dt>OAuth2</dt>
          <dd>An industry-standard authorization framework. Applications use it to obtain access tokens from an authorization server, which are then sent with each API request.</dd>
          <dt>JWT (JSON Web Token)</dt>
          <dd>A compact, signed token used for authentication. Contains claims (like client ID, audience, expiry) and is signed with a private key. The toolkit generates JWT creation code automatically when detected.</dd>
          <dt>DPoP (Demonstrating Proof-of-Possession)</dt>
          <dd>A security mechanism (RFC 9449) that binds an access token to a specific cryptographic key. Each request includes a unique signed proof – preventing stolen tokens from being reused by attackers.</dd>
          <dt>PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange)</dt>
          <dd>A security extension to the OAuth2 Authorization Code flow (RFC 7636). The client generates a random <code>code_verifier</code> and sends a derived <code>code_challenge</code> in the authorization request. Prevents authorization code interception attacks. Generated fresh each iteration — not correlated from a server response.</dd>
          <dt>mTLS (Mutual TLS)</dt>
          <dd>Two-way certificate authentication where both the client and server present certificates. Upload your client certificate (<code>.pem</code> or <code>.p12</code>) alongside your collection.</dd>
          <dt>CSRF token</dt>
          <dd>Cross-Site Request Forgery token – a server-generated value included in forms and headers to prevent unauthorized requests. The toolkit extracts and correlates these automatically.</dd>
          <dt>CSVDataSet (JMeter)</dt>
          <dd>A JMeter feature that reads test data from a CSV file at runtime. When converting a JMX file, upload any CSV files your script references alongside the .jmx.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

