  <!-- 12. FAQ -->
  <div id="hacc-faq" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-faq')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M9.1 9a3 3 0 0 1 5.8 1c0 2-3 2.5-3 4.5M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Frequently Asked Questions</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Click any question to expand</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-faq">
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">Which tool should I use?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">It depends on what you already have. If you have a <strong>Postman or Bruno collection</strong>, use the Postman / Bruno Converter. If you have a <strong>JMeter .jmx file</strong>, use the JMeter Converter. If you need to record a new journey and VuGen cannot record on your machine, use the <strong>Recorder</strong>. If you have a HAR file and want deep correlation (tokens, session IDs), use <strong>Script Studio</strong>.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">Which output format should I choose — DevWeb or Web HTTP/HTML?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">Ask your LRE administrator or project lead. If your project runs on LRE 2021 or later and there is no existing script convention, choose <strong>DevWeb (JavaScript)</strong> — it is the current default for new projects. If your team has existing VuGen C scripts or uses an older LRE version, choose <strong>Web HTTP/HTML (C)</strong>. You can always convert again with the other format — it takes seconds.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">Are my files stored on the server?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">No. Everything is processed in RAM and immediately discarded when your request completes. Nothing is written to disk, nothing persists between requests, and no other user can see your data. Download links expire after 5 minutes and are single-use. See the <em>Privacy &amp; Security</em> section for a full breakdown.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">My HAR file contains real session tokens and credentials — is that a security risk?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">The tool itself does not retain your data. However, the HAR file you export from your browser is a sensitive document. Best practices: <strong>(1)</strong> record against a test environment, not production; <strong>(2)</strong> use test credentials, not personal or admin accounts; <strong>(3)</strong> delete the HAR file from your machine once the conversion is complete; <strong>(4)</strong> never email or share HAR files from production sessions.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">My script runs but fails with "invalid token" or "session expired" errors.</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">The script has a hard-coded value from your recording session that the server no longer accepts — this is a missing correlation. The fix: open <strong>Script Studio</strong>, record the same journey <strong>twice</strong> using different credentials or test data, upload both HAR files, and click Analyze. The diff between the two recordings pinpoints every value that changed between runs. Accept the suggestions, click Apply &amp; Regenerate, then download the updated script.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">How do I get the best correlation result from Script Studio?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">Use two HAR files, not one. Record the <em>exact same journey</em> twice with <em>different</em> test data (two different usernames, for example). Script Studio compares the two and identifies every value that changed — these are guaranteed dynamic values that need correlation. After analysis, review each card in the Correlation Advisor — click <strong>Trace</strong> to see where a value came from, and <strong>Accept</strong> to add it. Then click <strong>▶ Apply &amp; Regenerate</strong>.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">What is the difference between the Recorder and Script Studio?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a"><strong>Recorder</strong> captures network traffic and converts it into a basic VuGen script — use it first. <strong>Script Studio</strong> takes one or two HAR files and produces a <em>correlated</em> script — it identifies dynamic values and generates the extraction and reuse code automatically. The recommended workflow is: record with the Recorder (or browser DevTools), then feed the HAR into Script Studio for correlation.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">My download failed or the ZIP is empty. What happened?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">The download link is single-use and expires after 5 minutes. If you waited too long or clicked the link more than once, it will no longer work. Simply run the conversion again — it takes only a few seconds. If the ZIP downloads but appears empty, check that you uploaded the correct file and that the conversion did not show an error message.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">The conversion completed but the script doesn't look right. What should I check?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">First confirm the file format: Postman must be <strong>v2.1</strong> (not v2.0). For Bruno YAML, make sure you exported the full collection. For JMeter, verify the .jmx was saved from JMeter 3.x or later and that all referenced CSV files were uploaded. If the script is missing auth headers, check that your collection includes an environment file with the relevant variable values.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">Can I upload files from a shared drive or network path?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">Yes — the upload areas accept any file accessible from your machine. Browse to the file on your shared drive or network location just as you would on your local drive.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">Why can't I record in VuGen on my virtual desktop or Azure machine?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">Corporate security policies and incomplete VuGen installations on some virtual desktops and Azure virtual machines prevent VuGen's built-in proxy recording from working. The <strong>Recorder</strong> tool is the solution — it captures traffic through your browser's built-in Developer Tools (F12 → Network tab), so no proxy or certificate is needed. Export the HAR from DevTools, upload it to the Recorder, and download the VuGen script.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">My application uses JWT tokens in pre-request scripts — will those be converted?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">Yes. PerfX Studio scans pre-request and test scripts for JWT signing code — including jsrsasign, jsonwebtoken, jose (JavaScript) and JJWT, nimbus-jose-jwt, BouncyCastle, JCA (Java/Groovy). It extracts the claim mappings and generates equivalent signing code: <code>lre-utils.dat</code> + <code>createJWTFromMap()</code> for VuGen, or <code>jwt-helper.js</code> + <code>getJwtTokenFromMap()</code> for DevWeb. Each request with a distinct claim set gets its own JWT call.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">My application uses DPoP — will the generated script handle it?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">Yes. If your application sends <code>dpop</code> or <code>dpop-pf</code> headers, the toolkit detects them automatically and generates the full cryptographic proof code (RFC 9449). For VuGen, it initialises the EC P-256 key pair once in <code>vuser_init.c</code> and generates a unique signed proof for every request that needs it. No manual configuration is needed.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">My application uses PKCE in its OAuth2 flow — is that handled?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">Yes. The toolkit detects <code>code_challenge</code> in authorization URLs and <code>code_verifier</code> in token exchange bodies. Because these values are freshly generated by the client on each login, the script generates a new PKCE pair at the start of each iteration using <code>crypto.subtle</code> (DevWeb) or <code>generatePkce()</code> in <code>lre-utils.dat</code> (VuGen). They are never correlated from a server response.</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="help-faq-item">
            <button class="help-faq-btn" onclick="hfaqToggle(this)"><span class="help-faq-q">My application uses Kerberos / NTLM / Windows SSO — is that supported?</span><svg class="help-faq-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg></button>
            <div class="help-faq-body"><div><p class="help-faq-a">Yes. The toolkit detects Negotiate, Kerberos, and NTLM challenges from response headers and generates the appropriate authentication code: <code>web_set_user()</code> in VuGen C, or <code>setUserCredentials()</code> in DevWeb. It also sets the runtime configuration (<code>IntegratedAuthentication=1</code>) automatically so no manual VuGen setup is needed.</p></div></div>
          </div>
        </div><!-- /.help-faq -->
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="help-noresult" id="help-noresult" hidden>
    <b>No help section matches your search.</b>
    Try another word, or <button type="button" class="help-mini" id="help-q-clear">clear the search</button>.
  </div>
  </div><!-- /.help-main -->
